            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "AIREDALER"
            },
            "reviewBody": "MY OPINION: Ditch the corn flakes and substitute crushed vanilla wafers and you'll like this MUCH better. Also... Use peanut oil instead of veggie oil since it has about a 50 degree higher flash point and you can fry these babies quicker at higher temp for less ice cream melt."
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "LatinaCook"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I enjoy fried ice cream very much. It is one of my favorite desserts. Although this recipe is very similar to what you would get at a Mexican restaurant, I've found a way to make this dessert without frying it and with much more flavor. In a medium heat skillet, I combine 1/4 cup butter, 1/3 cup flaked sweetened coconut, 1/3 cup chopped pecans, 2 tablespoons brown sugar, 1/2 teaspoon ground cinnamon and 1/2 cup flour. I stir everything until crumbly and golden brown. Then, I let it all cool down. I make the ice cream balls and roll them in the crumbly mixture and put them in the freezer covered until ready to serve. Then, I drizzle them with honey. I think this way is easier and has a lot more flavor.",
            "datePublished": "2012-05-07T19:36:35.773Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Cleo Gauthier"
            },
            "reviewBody": "This was great! I substituted the corn flakes for cinnamon toast crunch cereal and pulverized them very small. I find that this is the best way to fully coat the ice cream. I did two coats of egg white and cereal, freezing in between, and then I froze the finished balls overnight before frying. This way, they did not fall apart at all while frying. Do it this way, and you will have amazing results. My family absolutely loved it!",
            "datePublished": "2020-05-18T01:23:19.947Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "deesooman"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Easier to make than I anticipated. I pulverized cereal and rolled ice cream balls in egg then cornflake/cinnamon/cinnamon toast crunch mixture. Next, I froze them for a good hour. I then rolled them once more in egg, then the lightly crushed cornflakes and fried them. Finally, I topped them with caramel. No disasters, happy family. Kids have asked this one to be put in “the rotation.”",
            "datePublished": "2019-08-23T13:10:49.743Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Jena-licous"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I subbed a cup of crushed graham crackers for cornflakes. Added a few Tb of sugar to mixture. I had to recoat 3x to get a good seal for frying. Loved it with cinnamon sprinkled on top. Fried for 20-40 seconds. 10-15 was more of a raw exterior",
            "datePublished": "2016-11-30T18:11:00.927Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "ladybuggs5224"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I did use Cinnamon Toast Crunch rather than cornflakes because thats what I had on hand. I did freeze after scooping, double coated each ball, and put them in the deep freeze over night. I fried each ball for 20 seconds each and had no problems with excessive melting. The outer layer was nicely melted the rest was still frozen. We served in a cinnamon sugar tortilla bowl with chocolate syrup and caramel.",
            "datePublished": "2014-05-26T20:25:03.137Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Karen Sanders Fon"
            },
            "reviewBody": "This is so easy and amazing!!! Instead of vanilla ice cream, I used Breyer's Cinnabon ice cream and used half corn flakes and half vanilla wafers. So yummy!",
            "datePublished": "2020-06-13T01:00:29.120Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Karen D"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I use honey instead of egg whites and double the cinnamon, then put them back in the freezer. Coat the firmly frozen ice cream ball in honey, then roll in the corn flake/cinnamon mixture and put back in the freezer. No need to fry. That cuts down on the mess and the need to serve immediately.",
            "datePublished": "2010-03-18T11:29:05.760Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "ICEPIRATE"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Loved this. Crushed Frosted Flakes in blender with cinnamon. Coated the ice cream twice for a thicker crust. Served over tortillas that i heated in a pan with butter and sprinkled with cinnamon sugar. Drizzled hot caramel over the top and whipped cream.",
            "datePublished": "2011-03-18T13:12:46.520Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Cara Olsen Robson"
            },
            "reviewBody": "This was d-lish! I served the ice cream in a tortilla that I had coated in cinnamon and sugar, and baked into the shape of a bowl. Used a combo of cornflakes and graham crackers for the coating. Will be making this again!",
            "datePublished": "2011-02-20T04:33:03.963Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "laneej"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Sooooo good! Made for for Cinco de Mayo dessert last night &amp; it was a hit! I did 1.5 cups crushed Corn Flakes &amp; 1.5 cups crushed Vanilla Wafers &amp; 2 tsp. cinnamon instead of 1. Fried in peanut oil. Drizzled with hot fudge sauce, whipped cream &amp; a sprinkle of cinnamon. Will definitely make it again!",
